<p>University students in Iran protested again on Sunday across multiple campuses. Iran’s state news agency and other reporting described gatherings at five universities in Tehran and at least one in Mashhad. Several reports said demonstrators focused on memorials for people killed in earlier unrest, a sign that anger is still simmering even as authorities keep up a crackdown on dissent. Some of the protests turned into clashes, according to Reuters coverage carried by Al-Monitor, as Iran also faces rising pressure beyond its borders. Reports describe a U.S. military buildup and point to Iran’s push for a nuclear deal with Washington amid a tense regional climate. The protests show that domestic grievances remain active even as Tehran tries to manage high-stakes diplomacy abroad.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/hc1abP7rluirBFppbT32biI09lWbmMHjyZY6AaipCjmi_byE4lIl6T3lmggpSZiiRXIiO8ZidziaWoSEh5wX3IcxfymhHmxnGHNtC6gBruDCDThlH6qF6W2XHw53vJZ1ukY2zEMCSC1WzRshREDAG88HtOKcYNDix_6Wcil5SnQkzz-XNij_uigsq9M' alt='A screen grab from a social media post verified by The New York Times showing demonstrators at the Tehran University of Art on Sunday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Who is reporting what: Accounts varied by source. State media focused on where the protests took place, student groups framed them as continuing despite the crackdown, and Reuters said it relied on local news agencies and social media posts.</li><li>What the unrest follows: Reuters described the campus demonstrations as coming after last month’s anti-government protests in which thousands were reported killed, the worst domestic unrest since the 1979 revolution.</li><li>War-risk framing: Several outlets linked the student protests to heightened regional tensions, including warnings about possible U.S. strikes and the broader risk of escalation alongside nuclear-deal diplomacy.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iran state media: Iran’s state news agency said students protested in Tehran and Mashhad on Sunday, including gatherings around memorials for those killed in earlier unrest. (<a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/02/22/nx-s1-5722880/university-students-hold-new-protests-in-iran-around-memorials-for-those-killed'>NPR</a>)</li><li>Student groups (as reported): Student groups said protests continued for a second day at universities in Iran’s two largest cities, despite the state crackdown. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/22/world/middleeast/iran-student-protests.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Reuters reporting: Reuters said the second day of protests included clashes at several universities, based on details from local news agencies and social media posts. It also set the unrest against last month’s deadly anti-government demonstrations and a moment of U.S. military buildup as Iran pursues a nuclear deal with Washington. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/iranian-students-protest-second-day-some-universities'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/02/22/nx-s1-5722880/university-students-hold-new-protests-in-iran-around-memorials-for-those-killed'>University students hold new protests in Iran around memorials for those killed</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/22/world/middleeast/iran-student-protests.html'>Iran Students Protest for Second Day Despite State Crackdown</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/iranian-students-protest-second-day-some-universities'>Iranian students protest for second day at some universities</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/iran-protests-resurface-at-universities-as-war-risk-persists'>Iran protests resurface at universities as war risk persists</a> - straitstimes.com</li></ul>